No game content is redistributed
-------------------------------

This project ships no Baldur's Gate 3 assets: no textures, audio, models,
animations, localization data, artwork, icons, or `.pak`/`.lsf`/`.lsx`
data, and no copy of the game executable or any part of it large enough to
run or to reconstruct. It installs no file into the game and overrides no
file on disk.

For interoperability it does contain a small number of short machine-code
signatures — a few dozen bytes each — copied from the shipped ARM64
executable so the injected library can locate the functions it hooks.
These are listed in `src/Patterns.hpp` with the disassembly they were
derived from. They exist only to find code at run time and are not a
usable excerpt of the game.

Building this project requires no game files. The offline pattern
verification tool (`tools/pattern_test.cpp`) needs the user's own legally
obtained copy of the game binary, which is never distributed with, or
included in, this project.


What is written to disk
-----------------------

The injected library changes the running game process's memory to move the
camera. Those in-memory changes are gone when the game exits. The library
also appends to its own log file (`mod.log`) and writes a short status
file that the launcher reads and then deletes.

The launcher writes its own configuration and logs under
`~/Documents/BG3 Camera Unlock/`, and — with a one-time backup kept beside
the original — edits the player's own Baldur's Gate 3 input-binding
profile (`inputconfig_p1.json`) so the added camera bindings take effect.
It temporarily sets one Baldur's Gate 3 preference (`NoLauncher`) and
restores it afterwards. It does not modify the game executable, the game
application bundle, or any save file.


Reverse-engineered facts
------------------------

The memory offsets, structure layouts, calling conventions, and input
action identifiers used by this project are factual observations about the
behavior of a shipped binary, obtained by disassembling and debugging a
legally obtained copy for the purpose of interoperability. No Larian
source code is included; the work is based only on observation of the
released binary.


